
Controller tests on the host
============================

Run from the repository root with PlatformIO Core and a host C++ compiler
(Apple Command Line Tools on macOS):

    pio test -e native

For the standard IDE installation, use ~/.platformio/penv/bin/pio instead of
pio if it is not on PATH. PlatformIO downloads its native platform and Unity
dependency on the first run. No CoreS3 is needed and this command never uploads.

The test_controller suite uses Unity. setUp() creates a fresh RobotStateMachine
value for each test; timestamps are explicit uint32_t millisecond values and
press inputs are individual events, not held-touch levels. No real sleeps or
hardware mocks are used. The native build_src_filter compiles only the production
RobotStateMachine.cpp; Arduino main, RobotController, FaceRenderer, and M5Unified
are excluded. The firmware builds the same helper and supplies millis() and
M5.Touch.getDetail().wasPressed() at its hardware boundary.

Focused coverage: startup, the full awake state cycle, inactivity just before
and exactly at 30 seconds, press-driven timer refresh, press precedence at the
timeout, and no extra cycle without another press event. Each awake state has
three sleep/wake cycles, checking restoration and the refreshed wake timer.
Explicit timestamps also cover the inactivity boundary across uint32_t rollover.
Same-state assignment and repeated begin() semantics are covered too.
Existing initialization is intentional in this refactor: begin() resets the
clock to zero without resetting state, so pre-begin uptime counts toward sleep.
Assigning the current state does not refresh that clock. Validation evidence is
recorded in docs/plan/controller-unit-tests.md.

Firmware compile check (also no upload):

    pio run -e m5stack-cores3

Native tests establish controller decisions only. Physical touch detection,
renderer notifications and Serial output, face appearance, and blink recovery
still need the CoreS3 checklist in docs/agent-workflow.md. A build does not prove
those behaviors. Neither command above flashes a device.
